<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cardinal Richelieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1585">1585</a>–<a href="/years/1642">1642</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Armand Jean du Plessis, Cardinal-Duke of Richelieu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cardinal Richelieu was a French clergyman and statesman who was chief minister of Louis XIII from 1624 until his death. He strengthened royal power by ending the political independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> in 1628 and curbing the nobility, and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-one-hundred-associates">Company of One Hundred Associates</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu.jpg" alt="Cardinal Richelieu" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe de Champaigne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champaigne_portrait_richelieu_eb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Armand du Plessis was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 9 September 1585, the fourth of five children, and was delicate from childhood. His family belonged to the lesser nobility of Poitou; his father, François du Plessis, seigneur de Richelieu, was a soldier and courtier who died of fever in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> when Armand was five. In 1606 Henry IV nominated him to the Bishopric of Luçon, which the family held. Since he was below the canonical minimum age, he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for a dispensation from Pope Paul V and was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in April 1607. On his return he was heralded as a reformer and became the first bishop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to implement the reforms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1614 Richelieu represented the clergy of Poitou at the Estates-General. He rose by serving the queen mother Marie de' Medici's favourite, Concino Concini, and in 1616 was made Secretary of State with responsibility for foreign affairs. He became a cardinal in 1622 and Louis XIII's principal minister in 1624. His policy had two aims: to centralise power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and to oppose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, which ruled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1626 he abolished the position of Constable of France and ordered all fortified castles razed, except those needed against invaders, which made him hated by most of the nobility. In 1627 he personally commanded the troops besieging the Huguenot stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>.</p><p>Abroad he subsidised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and in 1629, alarmed by Emperor Ferdinand II's growing influence, he gave money to incite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He encouraged Louis XIII to colonise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> through the Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, and the Ordonnance of 1627 deemed Indians who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> natural Frenchmen. He also renovated the Sorbonne and patronised the arts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Near the end of his life Richelieu faced a plot by the marquis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-coiffier-de-ruze-marquis-of-cinq-mars">Cinq-Mars</a>, whom he had introduced at court. He suffered from recurrent fevers, intestinal tuberculosis and migraine, and he died on 4 December 1642, naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Jules Mazarin</a> as his successor. He left his library, with some 900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, to be open to scholars. Historians credit him with replacing a largely feudal political structure with centralised power, work that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> continued. He is also noted for authoritarian measures: he censored the press, kept a network of spies and had those who conspired against him prosecuted and executed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cardinal Richelieu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Cardinal Richelieu</b>, chief minister of Louis XIII from 1624 to 1642, and Cardinal Mazarin after him extended the use of intendants, royal commissioners in the provinces, and Mazarin survived the revolt of the Fronde (1648–1653).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Company of One Hundred Associates, created by <b>Cardinal Richelieu</b> in 1627, was meant to bring settlers, but English privateers under the Kirke brothers captured Quebec in 1629, and it was only restored to France in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cardinal Richelieu</b>, chief minister of Louis XIII, broke the military power of the Huguenots and the great nobles, and Louis XIV began his personal rule in 1661, on the death of Cardinal Jules Mazarin, governing without a chief minister.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Norman captain Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc landed on Saint Christopher in 1625 and, with the backing of <b>Cardinal Richelieu's</b> Compagnie de Saint-Christophe, founded a French colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-christophe">French Saint-Christophe</a></li><li><blockquote>Frédéric-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne joined the conspiracy of Cinq-Mars against <b>Richelieu</b>, and to save his life ceded Sedan to Louis XIII in 1642.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-sedan">Principality of Sedan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1622" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cardinal Richelieu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1622">What was here in 1622? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
